# Animated multi-agent pipeline hero — invariants

## Why this rule exists

The animated pipeline hero is the visual proof of ResearchOne's
differentiation — every competitor shows a static block diagram.
An animated, flowing pipeline that visibly hands off work between
agents *is* the demo: it conveys "this is a real multi-agent system"
in 3 seconds without copy.

**Redesign update (July 2026):** ResearchOne is evolving from a
fixed-stage universal pipeline into an adaptive research operating
system. The hero visualization must eventually reflect actual
orchestration — a stable core path with conditional specialist nodes,
optional skeptic/adversarial branches, and skipped stages shown as
visibly bypassed. The invariants below govern the current (Stages A–B)
and near-term (Stage C) implementation.

But animation is a place where well-meaning improvements destroy
accessibility and ship broken on slow devices. This rule exists so
the hero stays:

1. **Accessible by default.** Reduced-motion users get the existing
   static visual, NOT a degraded animation. Screen readers get the
   same `aria-label` and `sr-only` description that exist today.
2. **Performant on a 4-year-old Android.** No JS animation loop running
   off-screen. No layout thrash. No SVG filter chains that drop frames.
3. **Composable with WO-V.** The persona palette is read from
   `data-persona` on the wrapping section element; WO-W does not
   reach into WO-V's modules.

## Invariants

### I-1. Reduced motion renders the existing static visual verbatim

`useReducedMotion()` from `framer-motion` is the sole arbiter. When
it returns `true`, `AnimatedPipelineHero` renders `<HeroPipelineVisual />`
— the EXACT existing component — and nothing else. No animations,
no opacity transitions, no scale-in entrances. Zero motion.

This is non-negotiable. The reduced-motion contract is binding under
WCAG 2.1 SC 2.3.3, and the existing static visual is the validated
fallback shape. Per Rule 26 I-3 / I-10 analogy: when motion is
disabled, we get a known-good baseline.

### I-2. Static visual stays in the codebase as the reduced-motion fallback

`frontend/src/components/landing/HeroPipelineVisual.tsx` is imported by
`AnimatedPipelineHero` and re-rendered when reduced motion is active.
Label changes to the static visual must stay synchronized with
`pipelineLayout.ts` and `heroPipelineAria.ts` in the same PR.

### I-3. Core stages are canonical; specialist nodes are additive

**Current public framing (Stages A–B):**

The stage list displayed in marketing and the static hero:

```
Planner, Discovery, Retriever, Retriever Analysis, Reasoner, Skeptic,
Drafting, Verifier, Report, Recordkeeping
```

These labels are the product's public contract for existing audiences.
Do NOT rename, reorder, or collapse them without coordinating with
marketing — they are referenced externally.

**Adaptive orchestration (Stage C–D, forward):**

When the orchestrator gains specialist agents (market scout, competitor
mapper, feasibility architect, story verifier, contract auditor, etc.),
the hero visualization must reflect the actual agent roster for the run:

- A **stable core path** (Planner → Retriever → Reasoner → Report) always visible.
- **Conditional specialist nodes** render when the orchestration profile selects them.
- **Skeptic / adversarial branch** renders only when the intent requires adversarial
  review (adjudicative, causal_test, story_verification). It must not appear on
  informational, exploratory, or recommendation runs.
- **Skipped stages** are visibly bypassed or dimmed — not hidden entirely — so the
  user can see that a stage was considered and skipped, not absent from the system.
- When live orchestration metadata is available (e.g. `agentsToRun` from the run
  event stream), the visualization reflects actual selection, not a static diagram.

Until Stage C ships, the static 10-node diagram remains the canonical hero.
Do not ship a partial adaptive view that shows fewer nodes than today without
also exposing the "stage was skipped intentionally" affordance.

### I-4. Skeptic node is visually distinguished — conditional on intent

The existing static visual marks Skeptic with `animate-glow` and a
brighter ring per the strategic doc's "Skeptic is the differentiator"
positioning. When Skeptic runs (adjudicative and adversarial intents),
the animated version must preserve this: Skeptic node gets a brighter
background, larger ring, and beam handoffs in/out of Skeptic get extra
emphasis (longer dwell, brighter beam head).

**Adaptive behavior:** When Skeptic is not selected by the orchestrator
(informational, exploratory, opportunity-discovery intents), the node
should render as a dimmed/bypassed stage — not removed. Removing it
entirely would suggest the system lacks adversarial capability; dimming
it communicates that this run did not require it. Until adaptive
rendering ships (Stage C+), the static Skeptic is always shown
prominently as a marketing differentiator.

If you de-emphasize Skeptic universally (not per-run), you've broken the
marketing visual. Don't.

### I-5. Beam color palette is read from persona, not hardcoded

`PersonaAwareHero` passes `resolvedPersona={persona}` so beam colors
track React state when `data-persona` updates after mount (Codex PR
#112). Without that prop (tests, Storybook), `AnimatedPipelineHero`
falls back to reading `data-persona` from a DOM ancestor, then
`forcePersona` for harness overrides, then the default palette in
`personaBeamPalettes.ts`.

Per Rule 26 I-9: persona content is WO-V's responsibility, visual
variants are WO-W's. Adding beam colors to `personaContent.ts` or
copy to `personaBeamPalettes.ts` violates the split.

If `data-persona` cannot be resolved (rendering outside a
`PersonaAwareHero`, or rendering during SSR), default palette wins.
Never throw.

### I-6. Animation runs only when visible (IntersectionObserver gate)

`AnimatedPipelineHero` starts its beam loop ONLY when the component
is at least 25% in viewport. When scrolled out, the loop pauses.
This is cheap insurance against the "10 minutes scrolled to footer,
laptop running hot from off-screen RAF" failure mode.

Use `IntersectionObserver` directly, not a library. Pause by passing
`active={false}` into `PipelineBeams` (Framer `animate` becomes a static
hold). **Also** gate mounting of `PipelineBeams` behind client
`matchMedia('(min-width: 768px)')` (aligned with Tailwind `md:`) so
phones never run the infinite beam loop while the desktop column is
`display:none`. Keep the static node markup in the DOM for SSR/hydration
parity with the pre-WO-W layout shell.

### I-7. No SVG filter chains on the critical path

Drop-shadow / glow filters look beautiful on a developer's M3 MacBook
and drop frames on a 2021 mid-range Android. The hero must hit 60 fps
on `Moto G Power (2022)` or equivalent.

The beam glow effect is achieved via:
- A wider, lower-opacity stroke layer underneath the bright stroke
- Optional `mix-blend-mode: screen` against the lab-notebook canvas

NOT via:
- `filter: drop-shadow()` (40% perf hit on the relevant test device)
- `filter: blur()` (worse)
- Multi-layer SVG `<feGaussianBlur>` chains (worst)

If you reach for a filter, find a different way.

### I-8. ARIA stays intact

The wrapping element keeps `role="img"` and the exact `aria-label`
(`HERO_PIPELINE_IMG_ARIA_LABEL` in `heroPipelineAria.ts`, shared with
`HeroPipelineVisual`) and the same `<p className="sr-only">` description
that exist today. The beam SVG element gets `aria-hidden="true"`
because it is decorative.
Screen reader users hear the structure, not the motion.

The animated visual must convey the same information as the static
one to a non-sighted user. Per WCAG 1.1.1 (Non-text Content).

### I-9. Persona palettes pass color-blindness simulation

Every persona palette in `personaBeamPalettes.ts` is selected such
that the beam-vs-background contrast remains perceptible under
deuteranopia, protanopia, and tritanopia simulations. The palette
relies on BRIGHTNESS difference primarily, hue secondarily.

Test by running the palette through `chrome://accessibility` or the
"Emulate vision deficiencies" devtool. If the beam disappears, the
palette is wrong — go back to the drawing board.

### I-10. Tests must fail without the fix (Cursor rule 16)

Test assertions for this WO:

- `useReducedMotion()` mocked to true → rendered DOM contains
  `data-testid="static-pipeline-fallback"`, no `motion.*` elements present.
  Mentally revert the reduced-motion check — test fails.
- `useReducedMotion()` false → DOM contains the animated SVG and
  motion elements. Mentally revert — test fails.
- Section has `role="img"` and the existing aria-label text. Mentally
  remove aria — test fails.
- Persona attribute on the parent section → matching palette applied
  to the beam stops. Mentally remove palette lookup — test fails.

## Pre-commit check

- [ ] `useReducedMotion` is imported and used. Grep proves it.
- [ ] No `filter: drop-shadow`, no `filter: blur` in the animated
      pipeline modules. Grep proves it.
- [ ] `HeroPipelineVisual.tsx` is imported (not duplicated). Grep
      proves it.
- [ ] If you added a new persona, you added its beam palette in
      `personaBeamPalettes.ts` in the same commit. Per Rule 26 I-9
      composition contract.
- [ ] If you renamed any of the 10 canonical stages, you coordinated
      with marketing first. Grep `pipelineLayout.ts` against the
      marketing copy.
